{"records":[{"id":"fstsb2xfsf","title":"PML-regulated mitochondrial metabolism enhances chemosensitivity in human ovarian cancers","description":"High-grade serous ovarian cancer (HGSOC) remains an unmet medical challenge. Here, we unravel an unanticipated metabolic heterogeneity in HGSOC. By combining proteomic, metabolomic, and bioergenetic analyses, we identify two molecular subgroups, low- and high-OXPHOS. While low-OXPHOS exhibit a glycolytic metabolism, high-OXPHOS HGSOCs rely on oxidative phosphorylation, supported by glutamine and fatty acid oxidation, and show chronic oxidative stress. We identify an important role for the PML-PGC-1α axis in the metabolic features of high-OXPHOS HGSOC. In high-OXPHOS tumors, chronic oxidative stress promotes aggregation of PML-nuclear bodies, resulting in activation of the transcriptional co-activator PGC-1α. Active PGC-1α increases synthesis of electron transport chain complexes, thereby promoting mitochondrial respiration. Importantly, high-OXPHOS HGSOCs exhibit increased response to conventional chemotherapies, in which increased oxidative stress, PML, and potentially ferroptosis play key functions. Collectively, our data establish a stress-mediated PML-PGC-1α-dependent mechanism that promotes OXPHOS metabolism and chemosensitivity in ovarian cancer.","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2018-10-10T13:08:27.013Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/fstsb2xfsf","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/fstsb2xfsf"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"fatima","last_name":"mechta-grigoriou","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Yann","last_name":"Kieffer","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Geraldine","last_name":"Gentric","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Cell Biology","Cancer"],"journal_issn":["15504131"]},{"id":"zgtn6k2xsh","title":"Metabolomics in Multiple Sclerosis - Fitzgerald et al.","description":"De-identified metabolomics data from people with multiple sclerosis and healthy controls from \"Multi-omic Evaluation of Metabolic Alterations in Multiple Sclerosis Identifies Shifts in Aromatic Amino Acid Metabolism\"","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2021-09-10T13:21:12.424Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/zgtn6k2xsh","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/zgtn6k2xsh"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Kathryn C.","last_name":"Fitzgerald","orcid":"0000-0003-3137-0322"},{"first_name":"Pavan","last_name":"Bhargava","orcid":"0000-0002-7947-9418"}],"funders":[],"categories":["Health Sciences","Multiple Sclerosis"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"vp8d9f8rff","title":"Tumor-derived miR200a inhibits metastasis via Wnt5a/IL-6/B7-H4 axis in ovarian cancer","description":"Wnt5a/IL-6/B7-H4 axis was involved in tumor-derived miR200a inhibited-metastasis in ovarian cancer. B7-H4 in ovarian cancer cells and macrophages both exerted anti-metastasis effects, suggesting the important role of B7-H4 in ovarian cancer.fu","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2026-06-02T17:44:10.655Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/vp8d9f8rff","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/vp8d9f8rff"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"He","last_name":"Fei","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Medicine","Health Sciences","Oncology"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"dvv6kf856g","title":"EPICC","description":"The evolutionary dynamics of human colorectal cancer using single-gland spatial multi-omic profiling of DNA, RNA and chromatin","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2024-07-31T08:15:20.093Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/dvv6kf856g","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/dvv6kf856g"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"chela","last_name":"james","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Timon","last_name":"Heide","orcid":"0000-0002-1386-7469"}],"funders":[],"categories":["Genomics"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"ck4zbmz4h7","title":" A study of Targeting SCD1/FADS2 fatty acid desaturase alters lipid metabolic activities and redox-driven ferroptosis in ascites-derived ovarian cancer cells. XUAN Yang et al","description":"Malignant ascites in peritoneal metastases is a lipid-enriched microenvironment and is frequently involved in poor prognosis of epithelial ovarian cancer (EOC). However, the detailed mechanisms underlying ovarian cancer (OvCa) cells dictating their lipid metabolic activities in promoting tumor progression remain elusive. \nHere, we report that two critical fatty acid desaturases, stearoyl-CoA desaturase-1 (SCD1) and acyl-CoA 6-desaturase (FADS2), are aberrantly upregulated, accelerating lipid metabolic activities and tumor aggressiveness of ascites-derived OvCa cells. Lipidomic analysis revealed that the elevation of unsaturated fatty acids (UFAs) is positively associated with SCD1/FADS2 levels and the oncogenic capacities of OvCa cells. In contrast, pharmaceutical inhibition and genetic ablation of SCD1/FADS2 retarded tumor growth, suppressed cancer stem cell (CSC) formation, and reduced platinum resistance in OvCa cells. Mechanistically, inhibition of SCD1/FADS2 directly downregulated GPX4 and the GSH/GSSG ratio, causing disruption of the cellular redox balance and subsequent iron-mediated lipid peroxidation in ascites-derived OvCa cells. Hence, combinational treatment with SCD1/FADS2 inhibitors and cisplatin synergistically repressed tumor cell dissemination, providing a promising chemotherapeutic strategy against EOC platinum resistance and peritoneal metastases.","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2021-09-16T16:04:16.924Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/ck4zbmz4h7","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/ck4zbmz4h7"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Yang","last_name":"XUAN","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["RNA Sequencing","Western Blot","Liquid Chromatography Tandem Mass Spectrometry"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"63d44grrbr","title":"PP2A and CDK16 antagonistically regulate WIPI2B phosphorylation and neuronal autophagosome biogenesis. Tsong et al.","description":"In this study, we identified PP2A and CDK16 as regulators of WIPI2B phosphorylation and neuronal autophagosome biogenesis. To do so, we utilized a C. elegans reverse genetics screen and a visual readout of autophagy function to identify regulators of neuronal autophagy. We verified activity of PP2A and CDK16 on mammalian WIPI2B through in vitro kinase and phosphatase assays. Additionally, we showed that manipulating PP2A and CDK16 expression in primary murine neurons (DRGs) alters WIPI2B puncta formation and rates of autophagosome biogenesis. These data include western blots and fluorescent micrographs of C. elegans neurons and primary murine neurons. ","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2026-08-24T09:23:55.786Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/63d44grrbr","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/63d44grrbr"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Andrea","last_name":"Stavoe","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Confocal Microscopy","Western Blot","Image Quantification"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"rnfs539pfw","title":"CCMA primary multi-omics datasets","description":"We establish a single-site collection of 243 cell lines, including 207 paediatric cancer cell lines representing 14 distinct extracranial and brain childhood tumour types. We subjected 164 paediatric cancer cell lines to multi-omic analyses across three dimensions (DNA-sequencing, RNA-sequencing, DNA methylation) to classify them based on clinically relevant molecular subtypes.","source":{"id":"7628d440-43d3-4f11-9cb4-7b195c8180d5","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2024-04-19T10:35:52.387Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/rnfs539pfw","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/rnfs539pfw"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Claire","last_name":"Sun","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Paul","last_name":"Daniel","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Ron","last_name":"Firestein","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["DNA Sequencing","RNA Sequencing","DNA Methylation","Drug Screening","CRISPR/Cas9"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"x7zbyj6xh7","title":"Quantitative proteomic profiling of the human ovary from early to mid-gestation reveals protein expression dynamics of oogenesis and folliculogenesis.","description":"To understand the protein pathways important to human ovarian development, we performed a quantitative mass spectrometry analysis of human fetal ovarian tissue from 47, 108, 122, and 137 days of development as well as in healthy adult ovarian cortex tissue. By assessing these stages of development we were able to identify proteins that varied in expression from stages of ovarian development spanning primordial germ cell proliferation (at day 47) to oogonial differentiation and follicle formation (at day 137). We identified 3,837 proteins spanning these developmental stages (Table S1) and performed an SOM clustering analysis to identify proteins that were up-regulated between 47-137 days of developmental in comparison to adult tissue (Table S2). We also identified proteins that were differentially expressed between 47 and 137 days of development (Table S3) to identify pathways important in follicle development.","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2018-07-31T14:40:49.505Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/x7zbyj6xh7","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/x7zbyj6xh7"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Alisha","last_name":"Bothun","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Developmental Biology","Proteomics","Ovary","Human Development"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"f5p4h32p46","title":"Multi-modality deep learning model reaches high prediction accuracy in the diagnosis of ovarian cancer diagnosis","description":"This code supports the article \"Multi-modality deep learning model reaches high prediction accuracy in the diagnosis of ovarian cancer diagnosis\" .The weights are also be included.","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2024-02-05T06:29:43.161Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/f5p4h32p46","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/f5p4h32p46"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Yang","last_name":"Yang","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Ovarian Cancer"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"r8p67nfjc8","title":"Multi-Omic Pan-Cancer data from TCGA.","description":"Data consist of three omic blocks from The Cancer Genome Atlas (TCGA), containing whole-genome profiles of \n\n-Gene expression (file GE.RData), \n-DNA methylation (file METH.RData), and \n-Copy number variants (file CNV.RData). \n\nOmic profiles consist of information from 5,408 tumor samples across 33 cancer types (as matrix rows),  and 60,112 features (expression of 20,319 genes, methylation of 28,241 CpG islands, and copy number variant intensity for 11,552 genes). GE profiles by sample corresponded with the logarithm of RNA-Seq counts by gene (Illumina HiSeq RNA V2 platform). METH profiles corresponded with CpG sites B-values from the Illumina HM450 platform, summarized at the CpG island level, using the maximum connectivity approach from the WGCNA R package (Langfelder and Horvath 2008) , and further transformed into M-values (M=beta/(1-beta); Du et al. 2010). Omic blocks were adjusted for batch and tissue specific effects (see Gonzalez-Reymundez and Vazquez (2020)  and references therein for further details on quality controls and data edition).","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2021-01-11T08:34:51.289Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/r8p67nfjc8","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/r8p67nfjc8"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"agustin","last_name":"gonzalez reymundez","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Cancer","Big Data","Omics"],"journal_issn":[]}],"total_records":133001,"page":1,"page_size":10,"execution_time":125,"aggregations":{"data_type_stats":{"dataset":133001,"collection":0},"access_type_stats":{"open":132273,"restricted":68,"embargoed":660},"top_funders_stats":[{"id":"https://ror.org/01h0zpd94","name":"National Natural Science Foundation of China","count":2252},{"id":"https://ror.org/01cwqze88","name":"National Institutes of Health","count":669},{"id":"https://ror.org/03ha2q922","name":"National Science Center","count":472},{"id":"https://ror.org/02ddkpn78","name":"Fundação de Amparo à Pesquisa do Estado de São Paulo","count":419},{"id":"https://ror.org/00x0ma614","name":"Coordenação de Aperfeicoamento de Pessoal de Nível Superior","count":414},{"id":"https://ror.org/03swz6y49","name":"Conselho Nacional de Desenvolvimento Científico e Tecnológico","count":405},{"id":"https://ror.org/021nxhr62","name":"U.S. National Science Foundation","count":396},{"id":"https://ror.org/018mejw64","name":"Deutsche Forschungsgemeinschaft","count":328},{"id":"https://ror.org/00hhkn466","name":"Japan Society for the Promotion of Science","count":267},{"id":"https://ror.org/05r0vyz12","name":"Ministerio de Ciencia, Innovación y Universidades","count":227}],"source_stats":[{"id":"MENDELEY_DATA","name":"Mendeley Data","count":130374},{"id":"f881433a-b1cd-40d2-ab9f-4fd501f2d14b","name":"Elsevier Data Repository","count":1901},{"id":"a191461c-0227-403f-a1e9-621e0e0429cc","name":"San Raffaele Open Research Data Repository","count":184},{"id":"b07018a5-fee6-4df1-9d43-ff0d81ad75ed","name":"Bicocca Open Archive Research Data","count":131},{"id":"df228bbb-ac66-410f-8c66-f61608d63d95","name":"University of South Florida","count":75},{"id":"2a9d1b7d-e447-466f-888f-aa6f84e575e4","name":"Digital Commons Data@Becker","count":66},{"id":"72c28c45-627f-446b-8a48-af7c0a51a357","name":"Università Cattolica del Sacro Cuore","count":56},{"id":"b121dfec-bb51-4f19-a2f6-bc9ce0197286","name":"Babeș-Bolyai University","count":37},{"id":"7e51a2dd-133e-43c6-a220-12c1958e202a","name":"University of Canberra","count":32},{"id":"f2ed6517-7fb4-4e02-8cef-95e75e9f0c9d","name":"Embry-Riddle Aeronautical University","count":27}],"top_institutions_stats":[{"id":"3bfef41a-d03e-5722-bd55-dd245235fa78","name":"Bina Nusantara University","count":510},{"id":"0ad97c43-5173-56e6-bbe6-46dad5323862","name":"Daffodil International University","count":407},{"id":"f58d7a83-6efa-5d8a-a6c2-b9e78ee14783","name":"Maharishi Markandeshwar Institute of Physiotherapy and Rehabilitation","count":356},{"id":"55ba66b2-f276-5204-a3d8-acfa499794e0","name":"The University of Manchester","count":354},{"id":"48c8fa0f-965c-5cbf-a859-a5363432478b","name":"Universidade de Sao Paulo","count":303}]}}